L0241: Observation of a moved source

A proposition cannot freshly observe a place after ownership has moved away. A snapshot taken before the move remains meaningful historical logical data. Create the model observation while the source is accessible, then state later claims about that snapshot.

Example from `tests/corpus/reject/proposition_mentions_moved.lc`; the full file is a regression test.

```locus
// A proposition formed in a body cannot mention a local that was moved: the
// code no longer has the value. Evidence obtained before the move stays
// valid, being a value of its own; `snapshot!` (E8) keeps the value for a
// later claim. The generated Rust has no counterpart to the proposition, so
// this is a rule of Locus alone, not of rustc.
struct Token {
    id: u8,
}

fn consume(t: Token) -> u8 {
    t.id
}

fn afterwards(n: u8) -> u8 {
    let t = Token { id: n };
    let out = consume(t);
    prove!(t.id == n);
    out
}

// A lemma's argument is erased with the call, so it is a mention too.
#[terminates] #[no_panic] #[no_io]
logic fn id_of(t: &Token) -> Int {
    t.id
}

fn in_a_claim(n: u8) -> (out: u8, @(out == n)) {
    let t = Token { id: n };
    let out = consume(t);
    let claim = prop!(id_of(&t) == n);
    (n, prove!(n == n))
}
```

Language, as built: [1.12:3](../../atlas.html#/doc/language/spec-1.12:3).
